welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is eboo Patel the founder and president  of interfaith youth core he is at Yale  to give the coca-cola world fund lecture  at the McMillan center named by US News  and World Report as one of America's  best leaders of 2009 mr. Patel is the  author of the book sacred ground  pluralism prejudiced and the promise of  America and his 2007 autobiography acts  of faith the story of an American Muslim  the struggle for the soul of a  generation he is also a member of  President Obama's Advisory Council of  the White House Office of faith-based  and neighborhood partnerships today we  talk with mr. Patel about his new book  sacred ground welcome great to be with  you Marilyn thank you let's begin with  the organization you found it interfaith  youth core tell us about it and why you  felt it was important to found it sure  so the idea for interfaith youth core  hit me probably 15 years ago I was a  college student in the early to  mid-1990s at a time when the  multicultural movement was at an  inflection point and service-learning  was a big deal in college campuses also  and there were a lot of projects and  organizations that were bringing people  of different racial and ethnic  backgrounds together to do service Teach  for America city year and it kind of  occurred to me as I was deepening into  my own Muslim identity at the time and  as I was studying other religions and  their calls to serve others why why not  bring Hindus and humanists Muslims and  Buddhists Jews and Catholics together to  do service all of our religions call us  to serve others service is a great way  of bringing people from different  backgrounds together in a common project  so that was the against the original  animating idea of interfaith youth core  and it's it's grown into a really  ambitious organization and movement and  the kind of generational goal of  interfaith  Youth Court is to help make social to  help make interfaith cooperation a  social norm and the way that human  rights or that volunteerism or that  civil rights are social norms and we  believe that college campuses are kind  of many civil societies that can model  interfaith cooperation for the rest of  the country and that college students  can be inspired and trained and  mobilized to be interfaith leaders so  that's what we do at interfaith youth  core we partner with college campuses to  help them model interfaith cooperation  and we run interfaith leadership  Institute's for college students to  become interfaith leaders okay and then  do you track that the pattern out when  they go graduate and go out into the  world I mean do they continue that kind  of work that's a great question so that  is a part of you know to use kind of a  nerdy term that's kind of a that's part  of our theory of change this notion that  college students if they if they begin  to view themselves as interfaith leaders  during a formative stage in their lives  and their 18 19 20 years old and if they  have a chance to apply the knowledge  base and skill set of interfaith  leadership on their campuses by running  something we call a better together  campaign that that's their first steps  towards leading a life of interfaith  leadership and we have a really robust  alumni program at interfaith youth core  that helps college students do  everything from go on to divinity school  to study world religions and interfaith  leadership or to go on to be doctors  lawyers hedge fund managers washington  DC types who will take the perspective  of interfaith leadership into whatever  vocation or a career path they choose  and for us our definition of interfaith  leadership is pretty straightforward and  concrete it's an interfaith leader is  somebody with the knowledge based on  skill set to build understanding and  cooperation amongst people and groups  who orient around religion differently  and you can do that if you're a doctor  in a hospital you can do that if you're  a lawyer and a law firm you could  certainly do that if you're a pastor or  a rabbi or an imam you could do that if  you're in the business world and we  think that that's going to be one of the  key kind of frameworks and skill sets of  the 21st century in this highly  religiously diverse world in which too  often faith is a barrier of division we  need a critical mass  of people who are inspired and trained  and skilled to help faith become a  bridge of cooperation and can you give  me some examples of specifically how the  bridges are built and how people go  about working together that's a great  question thank you for that so this goes  back to kind of the the original  animating idea behind interfaith youth  core which is that service is something  that all of our faith traditions and  secular traditions call us to do and  service is a great common table for  people to gather around so what we what  we train students to do in our  interfaith leadership Institute's is run  interfaith service and dialogue projects  and I'll give you a great example from  my alma mater the University of Illinois  so the better together group and that's  what these groups tend to be called at  campuses across the country the better  together or interfaith an action group  at the University of Illinois would have  kind of monthly interfaith dialogue  meetings of their group and then every  year they would run something called the  interfaith an action day and for several  years it was modest in size like a  hundred to 150 students and a handful of  campus administrators which show up and  you know kind of was getting a start  like a lot of student groups do and then  a couple years ago there was that  terrible earthquake in Haiti in the  leadership of that better together group  at University of Illinois decided that  that they wanted to do something really  big and significant to help folks who  were suffering from that earthquake and  so they organized this huge interfaith  service campaign and project in the  university of illinois campus and  community that brought over 5,000 people  from different religious and secular  communities together to package over a  million meals for people who suffered  from the earthquake in haiti that's  amazing it it you know it improved lives  there in a very concrete way they got a  major federal grant to do it and for me  that's a great example of excellent  interfaith leadership because religious  communities and secular communities have  significant social capital volunteers  energy resources an interfaith leader  has the ability to mobilize that social  capital in ways that benefit a larger  community and so the interfaith leaders  that University of Illinois group were  able to go to the Muslim community the  Baha'i community the evangelical  community the Catholic community the  Jewish community secular humanist  community and bring them together for  this common project of packing meals for  Haiti and then they had this huge wall  where people could write their  inspiration to serve that was kind of  the interfaith dialogue part of the  project so there are biblical quotes on  that wall there are qur'anic verses  there are quotes from walt whitman from  secular humanism for me it's a great  example of an effective bridging of  social capital in ways that serve a  broader community and build interfaith  dialogue at the same time ok very good  let's move on to your book sacred ground  tell us a little bit about it and why  you feel it was important to write it  sure so in the summer of two thousand  ten America was caught in this huge  public discussion around the ground zero  mosque originally named Cordoba house  and the original vision of Cordoba house  was that it would be kind of a Muslim  YMCA an institution founded by a Muslim  community with with the resources of a  Muslim community but that was meant to  serve a broader public it was matt davis  swimming pool a culinary institute in  art gallery and it was meant to be based  in lower manhattan not far from where  imam feisal who was the founder of the  project had his head his mosque and  you'll remember that there was this huge  public discussion around this center and  it was it came to be called a terrorist  command center and Imam Feisal was  called a radical Imam and I remember  watching those news reports and reading  the papers and thinking that I'd entered  the twilight zone I mean I've known a  mom feisal for ten years I knew about  the plans for Cordoba house where they  were just being launched and and I  thought that this was actually a part of  of the Muslim process of of deeply  integrating in America in the sense that  our community was building an  institution whose primary purpose was  not to serve our own prayer needs but  was to serve a broader public's more  community or social needs  and so during that during that time I  started going back in American history  and looking at kind of the patterns of  religious prejudice and religious  pluralism in America and one of the  things that I discovered was that I  think across American history there's  been this dynamic of what i call the  forces of prejudice the forces that say  to some communities at different times  in history you don't belong right the  anti-catholic forces during earlier eras  the anti-semitic forces clearly the  racist forces in earlier areas of  American history saying you don't belong  and then the forces of pluralism the  force is saying actually the idea of  America is that we're a nation that  welcomes the contributions of all  communities and nurtures cooperation  between them those forces push back and  those forces have one era after era  after era and I kind of realized you  know we're in a new chapter of that  narrative of the the great narrative of  American pluralism versus American  prejudice and what this book is is it's  kind of a tracing of that of that story  across American history and in kind of  an explication of the chapter we're in  right now and it's a call to the forces  of pluralism of our era to stand up and  engage and defeat the forces of  prejudice whether it's anti Muslim  forces or anti-mormon forces or anti  atheist forces it is incumbent upon us  as Americans I think to recognize the  precious treasure of pluralism and our  nation's history but to know that that  it has to be protected and and and  that's what that's what sacred ground is  about don't you I think part of the  problem is the prejudice how do you get  beyond that how do you fix that so our  solution at interfaith youth core is to  is to first of all illuminate the story  of American pluralism I'll tell you one  one of my favorite parts of this when  George Washington first became president  he received a message from a Jewish  leader named Moses seshes effectively  asking whether his people Jews would be  safe in this new nation and Washington  wrote back a beautiful beautiful  document called the letter to the Hebrew  congregation of Newport Rhode Island  which said that America would give  bigotry no sanction and persecution no  assistance that's knowing the great  heritage of pluralism in America and I  think one of the one of the ways that  that the forces of pluralism in our era  gain strength is to know the heritage  and the legacy of pluralism in this  nation and they have a sense that we're  writing the next chapter in a glorious  story the skills of interfaith  leadership are really important as well  and I talked about this a bit in the  coca-cola lecture I gave yesterday  having an appreciative knowledge of  other religious traditions knowing about  the social gospel and Christianity  knowing about the role of mercy and  Islam we live in the most religiously  diverse nation in human history we ought  to know something appreciative about the  various religious communities of our  nation how they've contributed to to the  growth and success of America how their  values from Jainism or Buddhism are  similar to values and Catholicism or  Judaism it's it's it's something we at  interfaith youth core call interfaith  literacy and I think it's a key part of  being an effective interfaith leader  okay I think it's probably you know  there are some religions and some people  who you know believers who naturally  will work together but how do you handle  people who who are religious but are  just so filled with hate for another  form of religion how do you break that  down and deal with that yeah I think  that's a great question so in 15 years  of doing this work and and being in all  sorts of communities I've come to  believe that that the percentage of  people who hate somebody from another  religion is pretty small especially in  America right my sense is if you want to  think of this as a continuum there is a  there's there's a huge chunk of the  continuum that inclines in a really  proactive way towards being interfaith  leaders and then there's probably the  largest part of the continuum that's  relatively neutral or doesn't think  about this very often but certainly  opposed to it and then and then the the  part of the continuum that is opposed to  it is relatively small so the way we  work at interfaith youth core is to is  to look at the folks who are inclining  towards this and say you need to be  leaders who can go back to your  communities Geographic religious ethnic  racial etc and and convince them that  religious pluralism is something that  they have a stake in right so in a sense  what we do is inspire train and mobilize  young people on the on the end of the  continuum that is already inclining  towards this and it's a leadership  strategy right and there's plenty of  other strategies out there the one that  we're choosing the one that we're  betting on so to speak is is if we can  empower a critical mass of interfaith  leaders that will be the best way to  convince a critical mass of the nation  that protecting the precious treasure of  religious pluralism is something we  ought to be doing right and especially I  think it's important to their starting  as the their young people and as they  grow I think it will only help the  future of the country that's right you  know so so they'll have you know  god-willing 50 60 70 80 years to to be  advocates and leaders in protecting  pluralism right so if there is one thing  that you would like to leave us with  today what would it be what's the most  important thing you think we should take  away from our talk today I think it said  all of us need to be interfaith leaders  all of us need to recognize just how  important America's legacy of pluralism  is it is a remarkable thing that we live  in a nation whose very idea is to  welcome the contributions of various  communities and nurture cooperation  between them but that idea didn't fall  from the sky in the past and it it's not  inevitable that it remains the beating  heart of America we need a set of people  with the inspiration the knowledge base  and the skill set to protect that idea  and what that means is that we are able  to build bridges between individuals and  communities who orient around religion  differently and there's no more more  important time than  now America being the most religiously  diverse nation in human history the most  religiously devout nation in the West at  this time of global religious conflict  and I think about the the line from the  Parliament of the world's religions and  1893 in Chicago and I think this is a  kind of the mission statement of this  movement is is from now on the great  religions of the world make war no  longer on each other and instead on the  giant ills that afflict humankind sounds  wonderful thank you very much for being  here with us today i'm sharing some of  your work Thank You Marilyn for more  information about mr. Patel please visit  our website at yale edu slash McMillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the McMillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you  you

